      "latency_ms": 2497.6,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 484,
      "output": "At its simplest, **water** is the most abundant and essential molecule for life on Earth as we know it. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\nChemically, water is a compound made of two types of atoms: **Hydrogen** and **Oxygen**. Its famous chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. This means every single molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\n### 2. Physical Properties\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** What we drink, swim in, and rain from the sky.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water, which is uniquely *less dense* than liquid water (which is why ice cubes float in your drink).\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, clouds, and steam.\n\nAt standard temperature and pressure, pure water is transparent, odorless, and tasteless. \n\n### 3. The \"Universal Solvent\"\nWater has a unique molecular structure that gives it a slight positive charge near the hydrogen atoms and a slight negative charge near the oxygen atom. This makes it polar. Because of this polarity, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it’s called the **universal solvent**. It carries vital nutrients, minerals, and chemicals through living organisms and across the planet.\n\n### 4. Ecological and Biological Importance\n*   **For the Planet:** Water covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). It shapes continents, regulates the global climate, and drives weather patterns.\n*   **For Life:** The human body is made up of about 60% water. Every living thing—from the smallest bacteria to the tallest redwood tree—relies on water to survive. It regulates body temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and provides the medium in which all the metabolic chemical reactions of life take place.\n\n**In short:** Water is the lifeblood of our planet. Without it, life as we know it simply could not exist."
